# {{kebabCase name}} infra (Pulumi AWS)

Deploys the three apps (api, web, backoffice) to AWS as ECS Fargate services behind a
single ALB, with RDS Postgres and ElastiCache Redis. The platform (`@oss/*`) is consumed
as linked packages from the sibling `../igaming-oss` checkout, so Docker builds use a
combined context containing BOTH repos.

## Architecture

| Component | Resource | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Network | `awsx.ec2.Vpc` (2 AZ) | public subnets for ALB, private for apps + data |
| Edge | `aws.lb.LoadBalancer` (ALB) | 80 -> 443 redirect; HTTPS host-header routing |
| TLS | `aws.acm.Certificate` (DNS) | one cert covering api/web/admin hostnames |
| api | `awsx.ecs.FargateService` :3001 | Hono/oRPC via tsx; gets full runtime env |
| web | `awsx.ecs.FargateService` :3000 | Next.js standalone |
| backoffice | `awsx.ecs.FargateService` :80 | Vite SPA on nginx |
| DB | `aws.rds.Instance` (postgres 16) | private, app-SG only |
| Cache | `aws.elasticache.ReplicationGroup` | single node, private, app-SG only |

## Prerequisites

| Need | How |
|---|---|
| Pulumi CLI | `brew install pulumi` (or see pulumi.com/docs) |
| AWS credentials | `aws configure` / env vars with deploy permissions |
| State backend | an S3 bucket you own for Pulumi state |
| Both repos checked out | `../igaming-oss` next to this consumer (CI does this) |

## First deploy

```sh
# 1. Use your S3 bucket as the state backend.
pulumi login s3://<your-state-bucket>

# 2. Install infra deps.
cd apps/infra && pnpm install

# 3. Create a stack (dev or stage).
pulumi stack init {{kebabCase name}}-dev

# 4. Set the secrets (NOT committed - stored encrypted in the stack).
pulumi config set --secret authSecret <32+ random chars>
pulumi config set --secret dbPassword <a strong password>

# 5. Preview, then deploy.
pulumi preview
pulumi up
```

The `Pulumi.dev.yaml` / `Pulumi.stage.yaml` files ship the non-secret config (region,
domains, sizes). Edit them to taste before `pulumi up`.

## DNS / ACM validation (manual step)

The ACM certificate uses DNS validation, which BLOCKS `pulumi up` until the validation
CNAME records exist in the DNS zone for `{{domainBase}}`.

1. Run `pulumi up` once - it will print the validation CNAMEs (also in the
   `acmValidationRecords` output and in `aws.acm.Certificate.domainValidationOptions`).
2. Add those CNAMEs to your `{{domainBase}}` DNS zone.
3. Re-run `pulumi up`; the HTTPS listener comes up once the cert validates.
4. Point the app hostnames (CNAME / ALIAS) at the `albDnsName` output.

If you manage the zone in Route 53, you can wire `aws.route53.Record` +
`aws.acm.CertificateValidation` in `src/services.ts` so it self-validates (see the
`// AGENT:` notes there).

## Build-time public URLs

`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` (web) and `VITE_PUBLIC_API_URL` (backoffice) are baked into the
static bundles at BUILD time - they are passed as Docker build args from `src/services.ts`
(derived from `apiDomain`). Changing the api hostname requires a rebuild of web +
backoffice, not just an env change.

## Database migrations

Migrations are NOT run automatically. After `pulumi up` (and whenever the schema
changes), run them as a one-shot ECS task against the api task definition:

```sh
# From the consumer repo root - runs drizzle-kit migrate against DATABASE_URL.
pnpm db:migrate
```

To run inside the VPC against the private RDS instance, launch a one-shot
`aws ecs run-task` using the api task definition with the command overridden to
`pnpm db:migrate` (it inherits `DATABASE_URL` from the task env). Wire this as a CI
step before flipping traffic to a new api revision.

## Scripts

| Script | Does |
|---|---|
| `pnpm typecheck` | `tsc --noEmit` |
| `pnpm preview` | `pulumi preview` |
| `pnpm up` | `pulumi up` |
| `pnpm destroy` | `pulumi destroy` |

## Outputs

`pulumi stack output` exposes: `region`, `vpcId`, `dbEndpoint`, `redisEndpoint`,
`albDnsName`, `apiUrl`, `webUrl`, `adminUrl`, `acmValidationRecords`.
